+
+The configured plugins are commit-analyzer, release-notes-generator, exec and github. A [nonpublishing probe](release-path-probe.stdout.log) loads that configuration without running hooks, then exercises the **actual GitHub fetch wrapper** against a loopback HTTP fixture; it passes and resolves to **root undici 7.30.1**, a fixed branch version. Its [module trace](release-path-modules.jsonl) is retained. The absence of the npm plugin is one observation, not the security conclusion: npm ci, npm audit signatures, npx/libnpmexec, node-gyp, SOCKS and glob/minimatch paths are assessed independently above.
+
+Official release logs show **Node 24.22.1 / frontend npm 11.19.1** for both install phases, `npm ci --ignore-scripts`, `npm audit signatures`, and `npx --no-install --ignore-scripts semantic-release`. Those commands are distinct from the installed transitive npm 11.21.1. The [integrity-verified official npm 11.19.1 tarball observation](hosted-npm-frontend/observation.json) independently inspects that actual frontend: it bundles glob 13.0.6, minimatch 10.2.5, ip-address 10.5.0 and undici 6.28.0, all within the retained advisory ranges. Its ci/exec/signature and node-gyp/SOCKS source files are retained: ignore-scripts still gates install lifecycle execution, node-gyp still imports the vulnerable fetch/agents, and SOCKS Address6 inputs remain bounded byte-array conversions. No tarball code was executed or installed. These frontend copies are additional affected software on the hosted runner; the dependency audit counts above refer only to the selected project lock. [Node's official 24.22.1 source](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nodejs/node/v24.22.1/deps/undici/src/package.json) and [release notes](https://nodejs.org/en/blog/release/v24.22.1) identify builtin undici 7.30.1. The local probe uses older Node 24.21.0 with builtin undici 7.29.1, which falls within the five retained undici 7 advisory ranges. This local runtime was not updated and is not evidence of a vulnerable hosted runtime. See [runtime observation](runtime-observations.json). No arbitrary-host exploit or full release lifecycle was run locally.
+
+An [unapplied future-source patch](corrections/fail-closed-npm-audit.patch) inserts `npm audit --audit-level=moderate` after signature verification in the release workflow and reusable create-release action. This is a proposed fail-closed guard, **not an established repository policy, a vulnerability fix, an exception, or a change to published 4.39.0**. It audits the project dependency tree, not the globally supplied npm frontend or Node builtin modules; those require separate version/content qualification. [Exact YAML control results](proposed-gate-controls-corrected.stdout.log) prove both actual proposed commands exit1 on this locked tree and never reach a simulated continuation marker; both YAML structures are parsed and ordering/no-continue-on-error are checked. `git apply --check` passes against unchanged selected source. The initial control helper selected the source-guard job instead of the release job and failed; [that exit 1](proposed-gate-controls.command.json) and original helper remain, followed by the corrected passing helper. The existing release-lifecycle/credential contracts also pass against the proposed YAML files after restoring the unchanged required helper into the disposable capsule; the initial missing-helper exit 1 is retained separately in `corrected-release-contract.*`. See [passing receipt](corrected-release-contract-complete.command.json).
+
+A disposable four-leaf overrides experiment **does not remediate bundled dependencies**: lock generation exits0 but leaves glob 13.0.6, minimatch 10.2.5, ip-address 10.7.2 and undici 6.29.0 intact. The rejected candidate package/lock are retained under `corrections/rejected-bundled-overrides-*`, never presented as an acceptable patch. Current published npm 12.2.1 metadata still requests affected glob/minimatch ranges; merely switching to “latest npm” has not been verified to fix this. Fixing glob and minimatch requires major-version compatibility evaluation; fixed same-branch ip-address/undici targets are recorded above. All four vulnerable copies remain on disk.
+
+Remaining work is a separately reviewed future-source integration of an npm distribution/vendor dependency tree that actually contains the fixed leaves, regeneration of its lock, a clean script-disabled install and relevant CLI/release regression checks. The separately supplied hosted npm frontend must also be replaced or remediated and content-verified; a clean project audit cannot clear its bundled vulnerabilities. No successor SHA, producer version or corrected-source repackaging at 4.39.0 is proposed. The present assessment cannot certify all glob/minimatch inputs or all possible npm invocations; security acceptance remains false.
+
+The original `verify-proposed-gate*.py`, first replay, and hardcoded-toolchain reviewed replay programs remain immutable capture/history artifacts. Their executed outputs stay unchanged. The [portable reviewed replay](replay-proposed-gate-portable.py) retains byte checks, exclusive fresh output and explicit failure checks under `python -O`, resolves Node/npm from the supplied PATH (or explicit `--node-executable` / `--npm-cli`), and records the actual toolchain. These controls verify the proposed blocking guard only, not a dependency-security pass.
+
+[Current advisory metadata](applicability.json) uses `firstPatchedVersion`; the [original executed assessment](applicability-executed-history.json) is retained unchanged. glob 13→14 and minimatch 10→11 are major upgrades requiring API/runtime compatibility review and verification of the npm bundled dependency integration. Same-major targets for ip-address/undici still require an actual clean installed-tree check. No remediated producer version is assigned.
+
+Reconstruction requires Git, Bash, Python 3 with PyYAML 6, Node >=24.10 and an npm 11 frontend on PATH. These are execution prerequisites, not security clearance for that runtime/frontend. The commands below fetch only the selected source into new disposable scratch, verify its full commit/tree and selected dependency-file hashes, install the unchanged lock with lifecycle scripts disabled, apply only the proposed YAML guard patch in scratch, and run the reviewed guard replay with a fresh output directory. They execute no semantic-release hooks and mutate no real repository. The reconstructed dependency tree remains affected; an expected blocked audit is not remediation.
+
+From the Projects workspace root, the exact tested commands are also available as [reconstruct-and-replay-reviewed.sh](reconstruct-and-replay-reviewed.sh):
+
+```bash
+set -euo pipefail
+# Invoke from the Projects workspace root. Every Git write/install is confined to new scratch.
+for story64_tool in git bash node npm python3; do command -v "$story64_tool" >/dev/null; done
+python3 -c 'import yaml'
+node -e 'const [major,minor]=process.versions.node.split(".").map(Number); if(major<24||(major===24&&minor<10))process.exit(1)'
+npm --version | python3 -c 'import sys; raise SystemExit(0 if sys.stdin.read().strip().split(".")[0]=="11" else 1)'
+story64_security="$(pwd)/_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/evidence/6-4-advisory-resolution-20261021/producer-publication-verification-20261031/security"
+story64_scratch="$(mktemp -d /var/tmp/story64-security-reconstruction.XXXXXX)"
+story64_source="$story64_scratch/source"
+story64_capsule="$story64_scratch/capsule"
+story64_selected=2186b9a4bc507a116a457c590c80945c2e232cbd
+git init --quiet "$story64_source"
+git -C "$story64_source" -c submodule.recurse=false fetch --no-recurse-submodules --no-tags --depth=1 https://github.com/Hexalith/Hexalith.Builds.git "$story64_selected"
+git -C "$story64_source" -c submodule.recurse=false checkout --detach "$story64_selected"
+test "$(git -C "$story64_source" rev-parse HEAD)" = "$story64_selected"
+test "$(git -C "$story64_source" rev-parse 'HEAD^{tree}')" = 18d735e56eeb17332e2d2b57a6b116097f42fdec
+mkdir "$story64_capsule" "$story64_scratch/home"
+cp "$story64_source/package.json" "$story64_source/package-lock.json" "$story64_capsule/"
+python3 - "$story64_capsule" <<'PY'
+import hashlib,pathlib,sys
+root=pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])
+expected={'package.json':'6b24b99692d3ea18a3554e6018a271b951bd5a4c83db5895e1a83a98218d61a9','package-lock.json':'1ff1ac5a67d88d1c16eb10ac4b861e989d5c9c629f73a461ccc151c1f7e87724'}
+for name,digest in expected.items():
+ if hashlib.sha256((root/name).read_bytes()).hexdigest()!=digest:raise SystemExit('Selected input differs: '+name)
+PY
+env HOME="$story64_scratch/home" npm --prefix "$story64_capsule" ci --ignore-scripts --no-fund
+git -C "$story64_source" apply --check "$story64_security/corrections/fail-closed-npm-audit.patch"
+git -C "$story64_source" apply "$story64_security/corrections/fail-closed-npm-audit.patch"
+python3 -O "$story64_security/replay-proposed-gate-portable.py" --candidate-root "$story64_source" --capsule "$story64_capsule" --output-dir "$story64_scratch/replay"
+python3 - "$story64_scratch" <<'PY'
+import json,sys
+print(json.dumps({'scratch':sys.argv[1],'scope':'Affected-lock diagnostic reconstruction; no remediation, source publication, release hook or real-repository Git mutation'}))
+PY
+```
+
+The initial reconstruction refused the available Node 26 before creating scratch because its prerequisite was overly restricted to Node 24; its [original capture](portable-reconstruction.command.json) and [executed script](reconstruct-and-replay-initial-capture.sh) remain. The corrected portable script follows the selected `node >=24.10` engine range. [Reconstruction receipt](portable-reconstruction-corrected.command.json) records the actual toolchain and result; it is separate from the historical release runner identity.
+
+The earlier [executed reconstruction script](reconstruct-and-replay.sh) and its receipts remain capture-only history. The reviewed command preserves npm installation auditing, explicitly passes `--no-recurse-submodules` to the targeted fetch, and still runs the separate vulnerability guard against the affected locked tree. [Reviewed reconstruction receipt](reviewed-audited-reconstruction.command.json) retains the single fresh-scratch execution.
+
+After separately reviewing a genuinely remediated dependency tree and qualified frontend/runtime, rerun script-disabled locked installation, vulnerability and signature audits, existing release controls and full exact-source qualification at its independently derived version. Signature checks cannot replace the vulnerability audit. [Artifact hashes](artifact-manifest.json) bind this evidence package.
